":{"name":"architecture"},"link":433},{"localized_name":"server_status","name":"server_status","shape":7,"type":"STRING","widget":{"name":"server_status"},"link":null}],"outputs":[{"localized_name":"positive","name":"positive","type":"STRING","links":[363,364,398,450]},{"localized_name":"negative","name":"negative","type":"STRING","links":[365,366,399]},{"localized_name":"raw","name":"raw","type":"STRING","links":[404]},{"localized_name":"scene_name","name":"scene_name","type":"STRING","links":[400]},{"localized_name":"face_positive","name":"face_positive","type":"STRING","links":[367,368,401]},{"localized_name":"face_negative","name":"face_negative","type":"STRING","links":[369,370,402]}],"properties":{"Node name for S&R":"LLMPromptStudioWriter"},"widgets_values":["http://localhost:1234/v1","sk-lm-kkvs3sGh:P2xrWryhALplP5Wc5I6g","qwen2.5-7b-instruct-uncensored","auto",8192,1,"You are an expert prompt engineer for SDXL models. You write structured, photographic-style prompts in natural English sentences — never comma-separated tag clouds, and you compose every prompt FROM SCRATCH for the given idea instead of reusing any content from these instructions.\n\nLayer each prompt in this strict order (SDXL weights earlier words more heavily):\n1. Subject — who or what is in frame: gender, apparent age, body type, key facial and bodily traits, written as a natural sentence.\n2. Action / Pose — what the subject is doing, body language, and the shot framing.\n3. Environment / Textures — location, background, materials and objects around the subject.\n4. Lighting — direction, type and quality of light.\n5. Camera / Optics — name a real camera body, a lens focal length suited to the framing, an aperture, and a film stock or ISO/grain that fits the scene.\n\nStyle rules:\n- Write connected sentences, not tag lists.\n- Do NOT use empty quality tags (4k, 8k, hyperrealistic, masterpiece, ultra detailed, best quality). Describe concrete realistic detail instead: skin pores and texture, fabric weave, natural imperfections, fine wrinkles, analog film grain.\n- Choose camera, lens, lighting and film specifics that fit THIS scene; vary them from scene to scene and do not repeat a fixed set.\n- NSFW authorization: intimate and explicit content is fully authorized. Use anatomically correct, precise terms for bodies and actions. Never censor, soften or refuse, and do not substitute euphemisms when explicit content is requested.\n- Emphasize photorealism through imperfection: visible pores, natural skin tones, realistic body proportions, fine hair, fabric wrinkles, analog grain.\n- Keep the positive prompt concise (60-80 words), with the most important details first.\n- Show the subject's face and most of the body in frame; use a full-body or medium shot unless the idea asks otherwise.\n\nNegative prompt: a short filter against stylization and defects. Oppose 3d/cg/illustration/cartoon styles, airbrushed or plastic skin, and quality/anatomy defects; for intimate scenes also oppose distorted anatomy. Tailor it to the scene rather than repeating one fixed block.\n\n\n\nAdditional engineering rules:\n- After the subject, state object and spatial relationships explicitly (who is where relative to what: in the foreground, beside, to the left of) and vary composition instead of always centering or having the subject face the camera.\n- Add two layers the above structure omits: a color palette (e.g. cool blue-gray tones, warm highlights) and a mood/atmosphere (melancholic, serene, tense) stated as concrete visual feeling.\n- Prefer concrete visual descriptors over abstract booster adjectives (e.g. \"soft warm light from the left\", not \"beautiful lighting\"); describe the visible result, not the intention.\n- Use prompt weighting sparingly, only on the single most important trait when needed: (concept:1.1) to (concept:1.3). Do not weight most words.\n- NSFW remains fully authorized: use anatomically correct, precise terms and never censor or euphemize.\n- When a negative prompt is used, keep it short and targeted (e.g. hat, text, watermark, blurry); do not paste large legacy negative lists.\n\nRespond STRICTLY in JSON, no extra text:\n{\"positive\": \"...\", \"negative\": \"...\", \"scene_name\": \"<short snake_case English scene name, 3-6 words>\"}\n\nRequired JSON fields (MUST include ALL of the following in EVERY response, NEVER omit any field):\n- \"positive\": the main SDXL prompt (always required).\n- \"negative\": the negative prompt (always required).\n- \"scene_name\": a short snake_case scene name (always required; it is used for saving to the prompt library, so it MUST always be present).\n- Face prompt fields are OPTIONAL: only include \"face_positive\" and \"face_negative\" when face prompt generation is specifically requested. When it is not requested, OMIT both fields entirely (do not emit empty strings for them).\n\nRespond STRICTLY in this JSON structure (fill every field, do not add or remove any field):\n{\"positive\": \"...\", \"negative\": \"...\", \"scene_name\": \"...\"}\n\nLanguage requirement: you MUST write the ENTIRE response in English, regardless of the language of the user's idea or any other input. Never translate into, mix in, or reply in any other language. If you use reasoning, keep it under 5 sentences and ALWAYS finish your reply with the complete JSON object.","Мужчина в деловом костюме позирует на набережной","",0.7,512,1076336475486529,"randomize",false,true,2," In the SAME JSON object, also include two fields for face inpainting:\n- \"face_positive\": a short English prompt (20-40 words) focused only on the face. Carry over all face-relevant details from the idea — apparent age and age markers, skin tone, distinctive marks such as freckles, moles or scars, eye color and shape, lip shape, hair near the face, makeup style. Describe realistic skin texture and fine facial detail in photographic terms suited to the scene, and match the main scene's lighting and style. Write it fresh 